---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import SleepCalculator from '@/components/tools/datetime/SleepCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="sleep-calculator">
  <SleepCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the sleep calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        If you have a fixed alarm, use <strong>I must wake up at…</strong> and pick one of the suggested bedtimes.
      </li>
      <li>
        If your bedtime is fixed, use <strong>I go to bed at…</strong> and set your alarm to one of the suggested
        wake times.
      </li>
      <li>
        To go to sleep now, use the third tab. It counts from the current time on your device.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you know your own numbers, change the two settings: how long you take to fall asleep, and how long
        your cycles last.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What a sleep cycle is</h2>
    <p>
      During the night you move again and again through light sleep (stages N1 and N2), deep slow-wave sleep
      (N3) and REM sleep, then briefly come close to waking before the next cycle. One full cycle averages
      about 90 minutes in adults, which is the default in this calculator.
    </p>
    <p>
      Waking at the end of a cycle, in light sleep, usually feels easy. Waking from deep sleep causes sleep
      inertia: a heavy, confused grogginess that can last twenty to thirty minutes, however much sleep you got
      in total. The suggested times put your alarm between cycles.
    </p>

    <h2>How accurate is 90 minutes?</h2>
    <p>
      It is an average, and individual variation is large. Real cycles last from about 70 to 120 minutes, and
      they change through the night: early cycles have more deep sleep and tend to be shorter, and later ones
      have more REM and are longer. Age, alcohol, caffeine, stress, screens and sleep debt all change the
      pattern.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use the suggestions as starting points. If you often wake groggy at the 90-minute suggestions, set the
      cycle length to 85 or 100 minutes and compare. The setting exists because one number cannot fit
      everyone.
    </p>

    <h2>The fall-asleep allowance</h2>
    <p>
      Sleep latency, the time from lights out to sleep, averages 10 to 20 minutes in healthy adults, so the
      default here is 15. An error in this figure shifts every cycle boundary, so it matters.
    </p>
    <p>
      Falling asleep in under five minutes every night usually means significant sleep deprivation. Regularly
      taking more than 30 minutes is one of the diagnostic markers for insomnia.
    </p>

    <h2>How much sleep is enough?</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Age group</th>
            <th>Recommended sleep</th>
            <th>Roughly, in 90-minute cycles</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Newborn (0–3 months)</td><td>14–17 hours</td><td>cycles are 50–60 min at this age</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Infant (4–11 months)</td><td>12–15 hours</td><td>Not applicable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Toddler (1–2 years)</td><td>11–14 hours</td><td>Not applicable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Preschool (3–5 years)</td><td>10–13 hours</td><td>Not applicable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>School age (6–13 years)</td><td>9–11 hours</td><td>6–7 cycles</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Teenager (14–17 years)</td><td>8–10 hours</td><td>5.5–6.5 cycles</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Adult (18–64 years)</td><td>7–9 hours</td><td>5–6 cycles</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Older adult (65+)</td><td>7–8 hours</td><td>5–5.5 cycles</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The figures follow the National Sleep Foundation consensus ranges. Five or six full cycles (7.5 to 9
      hours) is the target for most adults, so those options are marked as recommended above.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting more out of the suggested times</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Keep the schedule constant. A regular wake time, also at weekends, improves sleep quality more than the
        timing of any single night.
      </li>
      <li>
        Do not lose an hour to reach a cycle boundary. Six hours of sleep is better than staying up another 90
        minutes for a "perfect" wake time.
      </li>
      <li>
        Naps work in cycles too. A 20-minute nap stays in light sleep and refreshes; a 90-minute nap completes a
        cycle. A 45-minute nap tends to end in deep sleep and feels bad.
      </li>
      <li>
        Light is the strongest signal. Bright light in the morning and dim light in the hour before bed shift
        your body clock more reliably than timing alone.
      </li>
      <li>
        Caffeine lasts. Its half-life is about five to six hours, so an afternoon coffee is still measurable at
        bedtime.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      This page is a scheduling aid, not medical advice. See a doctor about persistent trouble sleeping, loud
      snoring with daytime exhaustion, or falling asleep involuntarily during the day.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
